      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The snake horror subgenre peaked in the mid-70s with films like **Venom** (1981) and **Jennifer** (1978) carrying the torch into the next decade. **Fangs** director William Grefé, who also helmed <a href=\"/movie/67320\" class=\"movie-title\">Stanley</a> (1972), brought his Florida filmmaking experience with dangerous reptiles to create authentically threatening scenes. The practical effects and real snake handling set it apart from cheaper imitators, though the film's limited budget shows in other technical areas."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "What makes **Fangs** particularly noteworthy is its blending of environmental messaging with exploitation thrills, predating similar approaches in <a href=\"/movie/64976\" class=\"movie-title\">Day of the Animals</a> (1977) and <a href=\"/movie/30315\" class=\"movie-title\">Kingdom of the Spiders</a> (1977). While the social commentary may be heavy-handed, the film maintains tension through strategic use of real snakes and effective point-of-view shots that would influence later creature features. SUBHEAD: Legacy and Influence"
      },
